WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.265
Here's a tiny program.

2
00:00:04.265 --> 00:00:09.244
It works out how long a document takes to read, at 238 words a minute.

3
00:00:09.244 --> 00:00:13.157
It does that for 20,000 documents, and times the batch.

4
00:00:13.157 --> 00:00:17.069
Then it runs the same batch again, twelve times in all.

5
00:00:17.069 --> 00:00:18.563
Same code, same data.

6
00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:23.559
The first batch takes about a thousand microseconds.

7
00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:25.310
Then 713, 682, 482.

8
00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:26.876
A little quicker.

9
00:00:26.876 --> 00:00:29.549
So what will batch five take?

10
00:00:29.549 --> 00:00:30.747
Make a guess.

11
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:34.938
78 microseconds.

12
00:00:34.938 --> 00:00:38.175
Thirteen times faster than the first batch.

13
00:00:38.175 --> 00:00:40.132
And nobody changed a line.

14
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:42.726
Batch six is an odd one: 2,406.

15
00:00:42.726 --> 00:00:51.063
It showed up in most runs, even with the compiler switched off, on a machine shared by twenty busy programs.

16
00:00:51.063 --> 00:00:52.376
Call it a sneeze.

17
00:00:52.376 --> 00:00:55.000
One strange number is not a story.

18
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.004
The shape is.

19
00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:59.662
So here's the question that feels too basic to ask.

20
00:00:59.662 --> 00:01:01.342
Java is already compiled.

21
00:01:01.342 --> 00:01:05.107
So how can the same code get faster, just by running it?

22
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.587
Picture a new cook in a busy restaurant.

23
00:01:09.587 --> 00:01:14.373
On day one, every dish is cooked from its recipe card, one line at a time.

24
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.669
The cook also keeps tally marks: how often each dish gets ordered.

25
00:01:18.669 --> 00:01:21.506
Dishes ordered often earn a quick cheat sheet.

26
00:01:21.506 --> 00:01:27.548
And next to it, the cook takes notes on the orders: who wants extra sauce, who never wants onions.

27
00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:36.159
The house favourites get practised until the cook can make them without looking, with shortcuts based on what customers usually order.

28
00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:36.973
Same recipes.

29
00:01:36.973 --> 00:01:39.541
Just a lot faster by the end of the week.

30
00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:47.425
Now let's map the kitchen onto the JVM, the program that runs your compiled Java and Kotlin.

31
00:01:47.425 --> 00:01:52.039
javac and kotlinc don't make machine code for your processor.

32
00:01:52.039 --> 00:01:55.896
They make bytecode: small instructions for the JVM.

33
00:01:55.896 --> 00:01:57.409
lload pushes a long.

34
00:01:57.409 --> 00:01:59.375
lmul multiplies two longs.

35
00:01:59.375 --> 00:02:01.115
That's the recipe card.

36
00:02:01.333 --> 00:02:11.849
HotSpot, the JVM in every OpenJDK build, starts every method in the interpreter: a loop that reads one bytecode at a time, and carries it out.

37
00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:13.848
That's the cook on day one.

38
00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:21.742
Meanwhile, the JVM keeps tally marks: how often each method is called, and how often each loop jumps back to its start.

39
00:02:21.742 --> 00:02:28.513
When the marks pass a threshold, a compiler thread turns the method into machine code, in the background.

40
00:02:28.513 --> 00:02:30.577
The next call runs that instead.

41
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.763
Compiling while the program runs is called just in time compiling, or the JIT.

42
00:02:35.763 --> 00:02:39.581
Because it waits, it gets to see what your code really does.

43
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.545
HotSpot has two JIT compilers.

44
00:02:42.545 --> 00:02:45.543
C1 compiles quickly, and optimizes a little.

45
00:02:45.543 --> 00:02:47.111
That's the cheat sheet.

46
00:02:47.111 --> 00:02:54.948
At tier 3, its code also takes notes, called a profile: which branches ran, and which types turned up at each call.

47
00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:59.163
C2 compiles slowly, and optimizes hard, using those notes.

48
00:02:59.163 --> 00:03:04.055
That's the practised dish, with shortcuts that bet on the usual orders.

49
00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:08.007
So the normal climb is tier 0, the interpreter.

50
00:03:08.007 --> 00:03:10.793
Then tier 3, after about 200 calls.

51
00:03:10.793 --> 00:03:13.976
Then tier 4, C2, after about 5,000 more.

52
00:03:13.976 --> 00:03:15.568
Loop laps count too.

53
00:03:15.568 --> 00:03:22.412
When the compile queue is long, the JVM raises the bar, so treat these as rough sizes.

54
00:03:23.333 --> 00:03:28.042
Now, you may have read that a method gets compiled after 10,000 calls.

55
00:03:28.042 --> 00:03:29.388
That number is real.

56
00:03:29.388 --> 00:03:35.240
It's called CompileThreshold, and it only applies with tiered compilation switched off.

57
00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.931
Tiered has been the default since JDK 8.

58
00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:41.785
So those blog posts weren't wrong, just old.

59
00:03:41.785 --> 00:03:47.097
The internet remembers everything, even what stopped being true.

60
00:03:47.097 --> 00:03:51.828
Today's numbers are 200 and 5,000, from JDK 21 to JDK 27.

61
00:03:52.733 --> 00:03:56.444
Let's test the story by taking cooks out of the kitchen.

62
00:03:56.444 --> 00:03:58.299
First, the interpreter only.

63
00:03:58.299 --> 00:04:01.744
Then C1 only: cheat sheets, but no practised dishes.

64
00:04:01.744 --> 00:04:03.931
What happens to the late batches?

65
00:04:03.931 --> 00:04:04.991
Pause and guess.

66
00:04:07.967 --> 00:04:13.672
Interpreter only: every batch stays between 630 and 920 microseconds.

67
00:04:13.672 --> 00:04:16.897
This cook never stops reading the card.

68
00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.512
C1 only: 69 microseconds from batch six on.

69
00:04:20.512 --> 00:04:24.559
That's a touch faster than the full kitchen, at 78.

70
00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:25.115
Wait.

71
00:04:25.115 --> 00:04:27.555
C2, the expensive one, didn't help?

72
00:04:27.555 --> 00:04:28.183
Not here.

73
00:04:28.183 --> 00:04:31.180
That's a master chef, hired to boil an egg.

74
00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:36.339
On a one line method, the cheat sheet is already about as good as it gets.

75
00:04:36.339 --> 00:04:40.871
C2 earns its slow compile on bigger code, and lesson 2 shows how.

76
00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.651
And here's where the kitchen picture breaks.

77
00:04:44.651 --> 00:04:46.724
A real cook remembers yesterday.

78
00:04:46.724 --> 00:04:49.768
A JVM forgets everything when its process ends.

79
00:04:49.768 --> 00:04:52.813
So every restart is the cook's first day again.

80
00:04:53.733 --> 00:04:59.689
So the same code runs at different speeds, depending on how long the JVM has watched it.

81
00:04:59.689 --> 00:05:01.448
Never trust the first lap.

82
00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:02.952
Three habits follow.

83
00:05:02.952 --> 00:05:06.037
Measure after warm-up, or say it's a cold start.

84
00:05:06.037 --> 00:05:09.186
On a busy machine, read the shape, not one batch.

85
00:05:09.186 --> 00:05:13.107
And one call right after start is not the speed of your code.

86
00:05:14.033 --> 00:05:17.737
The moral of the story: your code is compiled twice.

87
00:05:17.737 --> 00:05:20.586
Once by javac or kotlinc, into bytecode.

88
00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:23.150
And again while it runs, by the JIT.

89
00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:25.358
The interpreter reads the card.

90
00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:28.349
C1 writes the cheat sheet and takes notes.

91
00:05:28.349 --> 00:05:30.415
C2 practises, with shortcuts.

92
00:05:30.633 --> 00:05:31.435
Quick check.

93
00:05:31.435 --> 00:05:35.710
A test starts the JVM, calls a method once, and times that call.

94
00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:39.784
Which tier most likely ran it, and what did the test measure?

95
00:05:42.967 --> 00:05:44.637
The interpreter, tier 0.

96
00:05:44.637 --> 00:05:46.935
It measured the cook's first day.

97
00:05:46.935 --> 00:05:50.624
Next, we'll ask the JVM which compiler ran, and when.
